claimed: intent-gate-non-english — the language table in the spec was measured with generic names + examples + gate, and the gate has since been demoted to a fallback, so the recorded numbers describe a mechanism no longer recommended. Re-measure per language on the current recommendation (domain-bearing names + examples, no gate) and keep the gated arm beside it, because the open question is precisely whether the gate still pays outside English: it kept Other recall at 1.00 everywhere but lost precision to 0.60 in Russian and 0.75 in French, pushing genuine meeting messages out of the domain. If domain names carry across languages, the fallback stops being needed where it was doing the most harm; if they do not, the language effect is about the model reading English domain words and that is a different and more useful thing to know.
